1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
In 1897, a chemist at Bayer in Germany

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,120
made a pure sample of acetylsalicylic acid, and

3
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:10,640
in 1899 it went on sale as Aspirin.

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:13,440
More than a century later, a school lab

5
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:15,760
can make a few grams in an afternoon,

6
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,760
and factories make it by the tonne.

7
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,040
So how do chemists decide on a recipe,

8
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:22,587
and how do you judge whether it's a

9
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:23,200
good one?

10
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:25,600
Here's the reaction.

11
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,240
Salicylic acid has an O H group on

12
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:29,040
its ring.

13
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:33,520
Ethanoic anhydride, also called acetic anhydride, puts an

14
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:36,320
acetyl group onto that oxygen, making an ester

15
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:38,400
link, like the ones in the esters video.

16
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:41,040
The other half of the anhydride leaves as

17
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:42,160
ethanoic acid.

18
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:45,280
So you get aspirin, plus ethanoic acid as

19
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:46,080
a by-product.

20
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:48,800
Every factor we judge comes back to this

21
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:50,080
one equation.

22
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:54,420
Before anything else, you need the reagents, in

23
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:55,260
bulk.

24
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:58,180
Salicylic acid was first made from compounds in

25
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:01,140
plants such as willow bark, but plants couldn't

26
00:01:01,220 --> 00:01:03,540
supply tablets for the whole world.

27
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:06,820
Industry makes it from phenol and carbon dioxide,

28
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:09,620
and phenol comes mostly from crude oil.

29
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:12,740
The anhydride is made from ethanoic acid.

30
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:16,420
So availability means a cheap, steady supply, and

31
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,540
here that supply traces back to fossil fuels.

32
00:01:20,767 --> 00:01:23,566
Why not use plain ethanoic acid, the acid

33
00:01:23,607 --> 00:01:24,406
in vinegar?

34
00:01:24,687 --> 00:01:26,846
It can make the same ester, but that

35
00:01:27,127 --> 00:01:28,606
reaction is an equilibrium.

36
00:01:29,087 --> 00:01:31,646
The mixture settles with plenty of salicylic acid

37
00:01:31,727 --> 00:01:34,286
left over, and the water it makes drives

38
00:01:34,327 --> 00:01:35,606
the reaction back.

39
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:37,966
The anhydride makes no water.

40
00:01:38,287 --> 00:01:41,246
It makes ethanoic acid instead, so the reaction

41
00:01:41,327 --> 00:01:43,486
runs almost all the way to aspirin.

42
00:01:43,887 --> 00:01:45,806
That means a much higher yield.

43
00:01:46,287 --> 00:01:46,846
The catch?

44
00:01:47,327 --> 00:01:50,046
The anhydride costs more, and it reacts with

45
00:01:50,127 --> 00:01:52,846
water, so everything has to stay dry.

46
00:01:54,067 --> 00:01:55,506
Next, speed.

47
00:01:55,987 --> 00:01:58,466
At room temperature the reaction is slow.

48
00:01:59,027 --> 00:02:01,986
A few drops of concentrated sulfuric or phosphoric

49
00:02:02,067 --> 00:02:04,546
acid act as a catalyst, and a warm

50
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:07,746
water bath, about seventy to eighty degrees, gives

51
00:02:07,827 --> 00:02:09,906
more collisions with enough energy.

52
00:02:10,227 --> 00:02:12,866
The catalyst gets you there sooner; it doesn't

53
00:02:13,027 --> 00:02:14,306
change where you end up.

54
00:02:14,627 --> 00:02:16,386
So why not heat it harder?

55
00:02:16,627 --> 00:02:19,266
A hotter mixture can make more side products,

56
00:02:19,307 --> 00:02:22,466
the anhydride fumes more, and the energy costs

57
00:02:22,547 --> 00:02:23,186
money.

58
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:25,586
Like the Haber process, it's a balance of

59
00:02:25,667 --> 00:02:27,746
speed, yield and cost.

60
00:02:28,967 --> 00:02:30,646
Now, how much do you get?

61
00:02:31,207 --> 00:02:33,606
Yield compares the aspirin you collect with the

62
00:02:33,687 --> 00:02:36,326
most the equation says you could make.

63
00:02:36,407 --> 00:02:39,126
Even when the reaction goes almost to completion,

64
00:02:39,327 --> 00:02:40,806
product gets lost.

65
00:02:40,967 --> 00:02:43,366
Some stays dissolved in the liquid, some sticks

66
00:02:43,407 --> 00:02:45,566
to the glassware, and some is left on

67
00:02:45,607 --> 00:02:46,966
the filter paper.

68
00:02:47,127 --> 00:02:49,046
So a yield well under a hundred per

69
00:02:49,167 --> 00:02:51,846
cent is normal, and in a factory, every

70
00:02:51,927 --> 00:02:53,766
lost gram is lost money.

71
00:02:54,967 --> 00:02:57,606
A medicine has to be pure, and leftover

72
00:02:57,687 --> 00:03:00,566
salicylic acid can irritate the stomach.

73
00:03:00,647 --> 00:03:02,646
So the crude crystals are dissolved in a

74
00:03:02,727 --> 00:03:04,966
little hot solvent and cooled.

75
00:03:05,047 --> 00:03:08,166
The aspirin crystallises out, and most impurities stay

76
00:03:08,207 --> 00:03:09,206
dissolved.

77
00:03:09,287 --> 00:03:11,606
That's called recrystallising.

78
00:03:11,687 --> 00:03:14,646
To check, add iron three chloride.

79
00:03:14,727 --> 00:03:17,206
Salicylic acid still has its free O H

80
00:03:17,287 --> 00:03:19,366
on the ring, so it turns the solution

81
00:03:19,447 --> 00:03:20,406
violet.

82
00:03:20,567 --> 00:03:22,326
Pure aspirin doesn't.

83
00:03:22,407 --> 00:03:26,366
Here's the trade-off: each recrystallisation makes it purer,

84
00:03:26,407 --> 00:03:29,526
but loses more product, so the yield falls.

85
00:03:30,734 --> 00:03:32,813
Then safety and waste.

86
00:03:33,054 --> 00:03:36,893
Ethanoic anhydride is corrosive and its vapour irritates

87
00:03:36,974 --> 00:03:39,533
the eyes and lungs, and the acid catalyst

88
00:03:39,614 --> 00:03:40,653
is corrosive too.

89
00:03:41,214 --> 00:03:43,053
So the work goes in a fume cupboard,

90
00:03:43,134 --> 00:03:45,013
with gloves and eye protection.

91
00:03:45,294 --> 00:03:48,333
The by-product, ethanoic acid, doesn't have to be

92
00:03:48,414 --> 00:03:48,893
waste.

93
00:03:49,374 --> 00:03:51,293
A factory can recover it and turn it

94
00:03:51,414 --> 00:03:52,853
back into anhydride.

95
00:03:53,134 --> 00:03:56,013
Used solvents and leftover liquids go into labelled

96
00:03:56,094 --> 00:03:58,773
waste containers, not down the sink.

97
00:03:59,054 --> 00:04:01,773
Less waste means less to treat, and less

98
00:04:01,814 --> 00:04:02,573
to pay for.

99
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:05,560
Finally, step back.

100
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:09,800
Environmentally, the starting materials come from crude oil,

101
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:13,800
and heating, solvents and purification all use energy.

102
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:17,240
Socially, aspirin is on the World Health Organization's

103
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:20,440
list of essential medicines, so a cheap, reliable

104
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:22,520
supply matters for people's health.

105
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,360
Economically, each tablet sells for very little, so

106
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:28,360
the route needs a high yield from cheap

107
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,120
reagents, with little waste.

108
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:32,840
Every factor pulls on the others.

109
00:04:34,067 --> 00:04:35,706
So how do you judge it?

110
00:04:35,987 --> 00:04:38,706
As in the extended responses video, decide the

111
00:04:38,787 --> 00:04:42,146
verdict first, then give each factor a paragraph

112
00:04:42,227 --> 00:04:43,346
with its evidence.

113
00:04:43,667 --> 00:04:45,346
Here's a judgement with a condition.

114
00:04:45,907 --> 00:04:48,306
The anhydride route is a sound design for

115
00:04:48,387 --> 00:04:50,946
a cheap, essential medicine, because it gives a

116
00:04:51,027 --> 00:04:54,546
high yield quickly under mild conditions, provided the

117
00:04:54,627 --> 00:04:57,026
ethanoic acid is recovered and the product is

118
00:04:57,107 --> 00:04:59,266
purified to medicine standard.

119
00:04:59,347 --> 00:05:02,946
Its weakness is its oil-based starting materials.

120
00:05:03,027 --> 00:05:05,266
That's an evaluation, not a list.

121
00:05:06,467 --> 00:05:09,346
One line to keep: yield tells you how

122
00:05:09,427 --> 00:05:12,066
much product you collected, not how good the

123
00:05:12,147 --> 00:05:13,026
route is.

124
00:05:13,347 --> 00:05:16,226
So when you evaluate a synthesis, start with

125
00:05:16,267 --> 00:05:18,786
the reagents, and end with the people and

126
00:05:18,867 --> 00:05:19,586
the cost.

127
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:21,720
Your turn.

128
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,760
A biodiesel plant reacts vegetable oil with methanol,

129
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:28,000
and the reaction is reversible.

130
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:30,640
It uses about twice the methanol the equation

131
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:34,160
needs, then recovers the leftover methanol and reuses

132
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:34,960
it.

133
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:36,960
Explain both choices.

134
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:38,400
Pause here to answer.

135
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:52,160
The reaction is an equilibrium, so adding extra

136
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:55,520
methanol, a reactant, shifts it towards the products

137
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:57,280
and converts more of the oil.

138
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:01,440
Methanol costs money, and it's toxic and flammable,

139
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:04,560
so recovering it cuts cost, waste and risk.

140
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,280
Saying the excess is mainly there to speed

141
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:08,800
it up is the trap.

142
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:11,920
The main reason is the equilibrium position.

143
00:06:13,134 --> 00:06:15,933
Last one, and it's a long response.

144
00:06:16,254 --> 00:06:18,813
A company near sugar-cane farms wants to make

145
00:06:18,894 --> 00:06:22,573
fuel ethanol, either by hydrating ethene or by

146
00:06:22,654 --> 00:06:23,693
fermenting sugar.

147
00:06:24,254 --> 00:06:26,133
Evaluate the two routes.

148
00:06:26,574 --> 00:06:27,933
Don't write the whole answer.

149
00:06:28,254 --> 00:06:31,133
Plan it: your verdict, then one line for

150
00:06:31,214 --> 00:06:32,173
each paragraph.

151
00:06:32,574 --> 00:06:34,013
Take a full minute if you like.

152
00:06:34,494 --> 00:06:35,773
Pause here to answer.

153
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:48,760
One strong plan starts with the verdict.

154
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:52,360
Fermentation suits this company better, provided it can

155
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:55,640
power the distillation cheaply, for example by burning

156
00:06:55,720 --> 00:06:56,440
cane waste.

157
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:00,280
Then one line per paragraph, each with evidence

158
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:04,360
from the card: supply, conditions and rate, separation

159
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:07,240
and energy, then carbon dioxide.

160
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:11,000
Listing advantages and disadvantages with no verdict is

161
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:11,720
the trap.

162
00:07:12,934 --> 00:07:16,213
So for any synthesis, ask where the reagents

163
00:07:16,294 --> 00:07:19,733
come from, which conditions balance speed, yield and

164
00:07:19,814 --> 00:07:22,933
cost, how pure the product must be, how

165
00:07:23,014 --> 00:07:25,253
safe it is and what it wastes, and

166
00:07:25,334 --> 00:07:27,533
who it serves at what cost.

167
00:07:27,814 --> 00:07:30,373
Then weigh them, and finish with a judgement

168
00:07:30,534 --> 00:07:31,493
and its condition.

169
00:07:32,374 --> 00:07:34,853
That's the last video in Module 8, and

170
00:07:34,934 --> 00:07:37,333
the end of the Year 12 Chemistry course.

171
00:07:38,534 --> 00:07:39,973
Here are the key notes.

172
00:07:40,214 --> 00:07:42,133
Pause the video if you need more time

173
00:07:42,214 --> 00:07:43,013
to copy them.
